## Standard Library

### console

```redblue
say "Hello"                      // print with a newline
print "Hello"                    // print without one
console.log("Hello")             // `say` under its module spelling
console.error("Could not read")  // diagnostics go to stderr
console.clear()                  // clear the screen
set name to input("Your name? ")  // read one line from the terminal
```

`say` and `print` are keywords rather than module functions, so there is no
`console.say` and no `console.print`: a module has the functions that are spelled
`<module>_<member>` in the registry, and those two are not. `console.log` is the
module spelling of `say` and answers `nothing`, so `say console.log("x")` prints
`x` and then `nothing`. `console.error` writes to stderr rather than stdout, so a
program that collects its output can keep its diagnostics separate from it.
`console.clear` writes the terminal's clear sequence to stdout and answers
`nothing`; on a stream that is not a terminal the sequence is still written.

`input` is the whole of the language's input: it writes its first argument as a
prompt, reads one line from the terminal, and answers that line as text with the
line terminator removed — `"abc"` from `abc\n` and from `abc` alike, since a pipe
whose last line has no newline is still a line. At the end of the input it
answers the empty text rather than refusing, so a program that runs out of input
sees an empty answer instead of an error. The prompt is optional:
`input()` reads a line and asks nothing. It is a registered builtin rather than a
module function, so there is no `console.input` — the same rule as for `say` and
`print` — and it takes parentheses like any other call.

There is no `ask`. It was a reserved word with no rule in the parser to read it,
which is how this section came to name it, and it is an ordinary identifier
again — `set ask to 1` is a program. Both spellings are refused: `ask("n")` is
`Unknown function 'ask'`, and `ask "n"` — which is not a call at all, because a
function is only called with parentheses — is `Unknown variable 'ask'`.

### logging

```redblue
logging.set_level("info")          // debug, info, warn, error or off
set threshold to logging.level()   // "info"
logging.set_timestamps(no)         // deterministic records, with no timestamp
logging.set_destination("stdout")  // "stderr", "stdout" or a file path
logging.debug("looking up a row")  // below the threshold: not written
logging.info("read 12 rows")
logging.warn("the cache is cold")
logging.error("the file does not exist")
logging.reset()                    // threshold off, destination stderr, timestamps on
```

A record is written only when its level is at or above the threshold. The levels
are ordered `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`, and the threshold is one of those
four or `off`. The **default is `off`**, so a call added to a library writes
nothing and cannot change what the program using it prints; a program has to ask
for diagnostics before it gets any. The threshold is set with
`logging.set_level` and read back with `logging.level`. `logging.reset` restores
the defaults.

The destination is `stderr` by default, which keeps diagnostics off the
program's output stream, and `logging.set_destination` takes either the exact
text `stderr`, the exact text `stdout`, or any other text as a file path. A file
destination is appended to, one record per write, through the same filesystem as
`files.write`, so a record written by one engine is readable by the other. A
destination that cannot be written — a missing directory, a directory instead of
a file — is a catchable `IoError` naming `logging`, not a process abort. On Unix
the same holds for a genuinely closed or broken `stdout`/`stderr` descriptor; the
suite pins that with a read-only descriptor and a pipe whose reader is gone. Other
targets write through the standard handle, which reports a closed or broken stream
as the same `IoError`; the suite cannot close a child's descriptor portably, so
that path is exercised on Unix only.

A record has the shape `[timestamp] [LEVEL] message`, or `[LEVEL] message` when
timestamps are off. Timestamps are **on by default**, because a log a reader
cannot place in time is less useful; they make a record nondeterministic, so
`logging.set_timestamps(no)` is the deterministic mode and is what a golden
output or a corpus comparison should use. The timestamp is UTC, to the
millisecond, in the form `2026-11-03T12:34:56.789Z`. A host with no clock — the
WebAssembly playground before `rb_set_clock` — writes the record without the
timestamp field rather than failing the run or aborting; the same situation
makes `time.now()` a catchable error, because a logging call still has a record
to write. The message is written exactly as given, including an empty message
and the newlines of a multi-line one; the record itself ends with one newline.

`logging.debug`, `logging.info`, `logging.warn` and `logging.error` each take one
message. The four are functions of the module, so their bare spellings are not a
second surface. The tree-walking VM and the bytecode VM share one implementation
of the threshold, destination and formatting, so the same program writes the
same records under both.

### text

```redblue
set upper to text.uppercase("hello")  // "HELLO"
set lower to text.lowercase("HELLO")  // "hello"
set trimmed to text.trim("  hi  ")    // "hi"
set parts to text.split("a,b,c", by ",")  // ["a", "b", "c"]
set joined to text.join(["a", "b", "c"], by ",")  // "a,b,c"
set holds to text.contains("abc", "b")  // yes
set starts to text.starts_with("abc", "ab")  // yes
set ends to text.ends_with("abc", "bc")  // yes
set swapped to text.replace("a-b", "-", "+")  // "a+b"
set size to text.length("héllo")  // 5
set wrapped to text.wrap("the quick brown fox", 9)  // ["the quick", "brown fox"]
set changes to text.diff("a\nb\n", "a\nB\n")  // a standard unified diff
set body to text.dedent("    one\n    two\n")  // "one\ntwo\n"
set greeting to text.template("Hello <<name>>!", {name: "Ada"})  // "Hello Ada!"
set padded to text.pad("x", 3, "-")  // "x--"
set aligned to text.align("x", 5, "right")  // "    x"
```

`length` counts **characters**, where a character is one Unicode scalar value:
`length("héllo")` is 5 and `length("世界")` is 2. It is not a glyph count —
`é`, an `e` and a combining acute accent, is one glyph, two characters and a
length of 2. The **byte** count is still available and is spelled as a list by
`length(bytes.from_text("héllo"))`, which is 6, so a program that wants the
byte values rather than the count asks § bytes below for them.
`tests/test_text.rb` pins both numbers in six tests and
`corpus/unicode-0001..0008` record the character counts, including the case the
rule exists for.

`length` answers a text and a list, and it is the same function under each of the
three spellings: `length(xs)`, `text.length(xs)` and `list.length(xs)`. A record is
not a length — `length({a: 1})` is `RuntimeError: length requires a list or text`,
in all three spellings, and `tests/test_text.rb` pins the refusal as a caught
error. Both dotted spellings are module members, so both are written
above and in README.md — a name that answers without a document saying so is
surface nobody agreed to.

`wrap` answers a list of lines no longer than the width. A word is moved to the
next line whole; only a word that is itself longer than the width is broken, into
width-sized pieces, so every line the function answers is within the width.
Existing newlines start a new line, CRLF is read as a line ending, and an empty
text wraps to an empty list. Horizontal whitespace separates words and is
normalised: a run of spaces, a tab or any other Unicode whitespace becomes the
single space between two words rather than being preserved. The width is a whole
number of characters, at least 1 and at most 1,000,000; anything else is a
catchable `RuntimeError` naming `wrap`.

`diff` answers a **standard unified diff** between its two texts, with
`--- original` and `+++ changed` headers and three lines of context around each
change. Identical texts answer the empty text. A text whose last line has no
newline is written with the standard `\ No newline at end of file` marker, so
the output can be applied by any unified-diff reader. A text longer than
1,000,000 lines is refused, as is a pair whose line counts multiply past 4,000,000, with a catchable `RuntimeError` naming `diff`.

`dedent` removes the longest leading whitespace that every non-blank line
shares. A blank line does not vote, and a non-blank line with no leading
whitespace makes the shared prefix empty, which leaves every line exactly as it
was. Line endings are preserved, so CRLF input stays CRLF.

`template` replaces `<<name>>` placeholders with fields of its record. The
delimiters are literal text rather than braces because `{…}` already means
interpolation inside a Redblue string. `<<<<` writes a literal `<<`, so a
template can contain its own opening delimiter; `>>` outside a placeholder needs
no escape. An unknown placeholder is a catchable `RuntimeError` that names the
missing field. Values are written with their usual text form, so a text is
inserted without quotes and a number as its number.

`pad` right-pads a text to a width with a fill of one or more characters, and
`align` pads to a width on the named side: `"left"`, `"right"` or `"center"`.
Both count **characters**, not bytes, so padding `"é"` to 3 adds two characters
and padding `"日本"` to 5 adds three, regardless of their UTF-8 length. A width
past 1,000,000 is refused, and text already at or past the width is answered
unchanged. An empty fill and an unknown side are catchable errors naming the function.

These six are spelled as module members only — `text.wrap`, `text.diff` and the rest — so a
bare `wrap(x, n)` remains an ordinary name a program may declare. The earlier text functions keep their flat spellings.

### math

```redblue
set pi to PI
set e to E
set absolute to math.abs(-3)   // 3
set floored to math.floor(1.7)  // 1
set ceiling to math.ceil(1.2)  // 2
set sqrt2 to math.sqrt(2)
set rounded to math.round(3.7)  // 4
set powered to math.pow(2, 10)  // 1024
set s to math.sin(0)  // 0
set c to math.cos(0)  // 1
set t to math.tan(0)  // 0
set lg to math.log(1)  // 0
set raised to math.exp(0)  // 1
```

`PI` and `E` are globals, not module members: a module has functions, so
`math.PI` is not a name in the language.

`math.random` is not implemented. The random builtins are spelled flat: `random`,
`random_seed`, `random_number`, `random_choice` and `random_shuffle`.

```redblue
random_seed(42)                // every draw after this is reproducible
set n to random(1, 6)          // a whole number from 1 to 6, both ends included
set f to random_number(0, 1)   // a number from 0 up to (not including) 1
set one to random_choice(xs)    // one member of a list
set mixed to random_shuffle(xs) // the same members, in a seeded order
```

`random_seed` makes the draws after it reproducible: the same seed gives the
same sequence of draws, in one process or in two. A program that never seeds
draws from a fixed default seed rather than from the clock, so Redblue's output
is reproducible by default and a program has to *ask* for variation it cannot
reproduce. A seed outside the range of a 64-bit machine integer has no bits left
to give and takes the nearest bound, so `random_seed(1e300)` is the seed
`random_seed(9223372036854775807)` names; the two ends are different seeds from
each other. `random` answers a whole number and `random_number` a fraction; both
refuse a range whose low end is above its high end, because such a range has no
member. A range may be written with two arguments, with one — which is the high
end, with zero as the low end, so `random(6)` is a die numbered zero through six
and `random_number(10)` is `[0, 10)` — or with none, which is `[0, 100]` for
`random` and `[0, 1)` for `random_number`. `random` answers one of the whole
numbers in its range, so a fractional bound narrows the range rather than making
the draw a fraction: `random(0, 100.5)` answers `0` to `100`, and a range holding
no whole number at all, such as `random(0.2, 0.8)`, is refused. `random_choice`
refuses an empty list, since it has no member to choose.

### files and path

```redblue
set content to files.read("data.rb")
might fail files.write("output.rb", content)
might fail files.append("log.rb", "new line")
set lines to files.lines("data.rb")  // one entry per line
files.copy("data.rb", "copy.rb")
files.rename("copy.rb", "moved.rb")
files.delete("moved.rb")
if files.exists("config.rb")
    // ...
end
```

```redblue
set folder to path.parent("data/records.rb")    // "data"
set name to path.filename("data/records.rb")    // "records.rb"
set stem to path.stem("data/records.rb")        // "records"
set kind to path.extension("data/records.rb")   // "rb"
set full to path.join("data", "records.rb")     // data<separator>records.rb
set absolute to path.is_absolute(full)          // no
set here to path.resolve("data")                // an absolute path
set cleaned to path.normalize("data/./records") // data<separator>records
set entries to path.list(".")                   // sorted entry names
```

`path.join` takes its separator from the platform, so the same program builds
`data/records.rb` on POSIX and `data\records.rb` on Windows. It takes one or more
parts, and an absolute part discards everything joined before it: joining
`"data"` and `"/records.rb"` is `"/records.rb"`, not `"data//records.rb"`. On
Windows a part that carries a drive but no root, such as `"D:other"`, also
replaces everything before it, as the host's `PathBuf::push` does. A path
value is accepted wherever these functions take a path, and `files.read`,
`files.write`, `files.append`, `files.exists`, `files.lines`, `files.delete`,
`files.copy`, `files.rename` and `bytes.write` accept either a path value or the
text form every existing program writes.

`path.parent` answers `nothing` for a root such as `/`, and for a bare filename
that has no directory part. A trailing separator names a directory without
changing its last name, so `path.filename("data/records/")` is `"records"` and
`path.stem` and `path.extension` read that same name. `path.resolve` makes a path
absolute against the working directory and removes `.` and `..` segments
lexically; it does not consult the filesystem, so it answers for a path that does
not exist yet. The WebAssembly playground has no working directory to resolve
against, so there `path.resolve` is refused with an `IoError` that names the
platform; the native `rb` binary performs the resolution described here.
`path.normalize` performs the same `.` and `..` folding and separator collapsing
as `path.resolve`, but it does not consult the working directory, so a relative
path stays relative: `path.normalize("data/./records")` is `data<separator>records`
and `path.normalize("./")` is `.`. A leading `..` that cannot fold into a
component stays at the front of a relative path, and `..` cannot climb above a
root. The separator comes from the platform, as it does for `path.join`.
`path.list` answers the names directly inside a directory, sorted, so the same
directory always prints the same list; an empty directory is an empty list rather
than an error, and a directory that does not exist is an `IoError`.

`files.copy` and `files.rename` refuse rather than write half a file: a copy
whose source is not there, or a rename onto a path that cannot be written, is an
`IoError` naming the path. `files.rename` moves as well as renames, so a copy
written to a temporary path and then renamed is one file at the end rather than
two.

### list

```redblue
set doubled to list.map([1, 2, 3], to (x) give back x * 2)
set size to list.length([1, 2, 3])  // 3
```

`filter` and `reduce` are registered as flat builtins and neither is
implemented, so neither is a member of `list`: `list.filter(xs, f)` is
`Module 'list' has no function 'filter'`, the same refusal as `math.random`
above. A builtin that cannot answer is not a module member — the alternative is a
spelling the analyzer accepts and the runtime can only refuse.

`list.map` is the only higher-order builtin, and it works in three spellings:
`map(xs, f)`, `list.map(xs, f)` and `[1, 2, 3].map(f)`. `list.length` is `length`
under its module spelling, as § text writes it for a text.

The third spelling takes its receiver **literally**. A dotted call resolves its
receiver against modules and objects, and a list held in a variable is a value
rather than either of those, so `xs.map(f)` is refused as
`Module 'xs' has no function 'map'` — the same refusal `list.filter` gives above.
A list written out in full is the only one a `.` can be put in front of.

### network

```redblue
set response to network.get("https://api.example.com")
set response to network.post("https://api.example.com", data)
```

Both calls block until the response arrives, so there is nothing to write in
front of them. `wait` is an ordinary identifier, and the `wait`-prefixed spelling
this section used to carry did not parse. See "Asynchronous Programming" above.

### json and csv

```redblue
set obj to json.parse("{ \"name\": \"Alice\" }")  // {name: Alice}
set text to json.stringify(obj)                   // {"name": "Alice"}
set rows to csv.parse("name,age\nAlice,30")       // [[name, age], [Alice, 30]]
set report to csv.stringify(rows)                 // "name,age\nAlice,30"
set lines to json.stringify_lines([obj])          // one JSON value per line
set decoded to json.parse_lines(lines)            // [obj]
set config to toml.parse("name = \"Ada\"\n")      // {name: Ada}
```

A text is written between double quotes, and a quote *inside* one is escaped
`\"` — there are no single-quoted strings, so `'{"name": "Alice"}'` is a
`LexerError` at the first quote rather than a JSON document.

There is no `formats` module. JSON, CSV, TOML and the archive formats are
their own modules, spelled `json.*`, `csv.*`, `toml.*`, `zip.*` and `tar.*`.
